{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThis ticket was filed 45 seconds apart from `classless-dainty-lamprey` (039b-d179-b669-4439) by\nthe same scan and shares its title. 039b landed the FIRST pass as commit a79ed876b0: the\nsnapshot slow-progress test now injects a 1s low-speed window over a 2s real loopback dribble,\nand the ACLI read-retry test now installs 0.1s call/grace/drain, records the logical `[2, 4]`\nbackoff without sleeping it, and polls every spawned PID to absence. That work is done and is\nnot re-litigated here.\n\nWhat remains on current `origin/main` (measured, macOS, `-p no:randomly`, 20 tests / 12.67s):\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py` still carries a BLANKET\n  autouse `_short_timeout` fixture that sets `REBAR_ACLI_TIMEOUT=1` plus 1s grace and 1s drain\n  for the whole module. Five real-subprocess reap tests each burn ~1.01s of pure wall-clock\n  waiting for that deadline to expire: `test_grandchild_process_group_reaped`,\n  `test_write_not_retried_on_timeout`, `test_truncated_utf8_does_not_crash_reap_path`,\n  `test_partial_stdout_captured_on_timeout`,\n  `test_check_mutation_failure_not_called_on_killed_child`.\n- The same blanket fixture also caps `test_run_acli_429_retries_with_rate_limit_backoff`, whose\n  fake child must COMPLETE (two sequential `python -c` spawns) inside that 1s. That is a latent\n  load flake in the opposite direction, and naively shrinking the module-wide value would make\n  it worse — so the fixture must be split, not merely retuned.\n- `tests/unit/test_snapshot_fetch_stall_abort_12e4.py`'s `tight_stall` fixture still injects a\n  2s low-speed window (`test_stalled_remote_aborts_on_throughput_not_the_wall_clock`, ~2.06s)\n  even though its sibling dribble test already demonstrates that git honors a 1s window.\n\nThe residual coupling is not only \"the numbers are large\". Three of the reap tests assert on\nsomething the child must have DONE before the deadline fires (a grandchild pidfile, a spawn\ncounter, flushed partial stdout). Today the ordering holds only because 1s happens to exceed\ninterpreter startup — a wall-clock race that a smaller window would lose on a loaded runner.\nThe fix is to make the ordering explicit rather than to pick a luckier number.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Split the blanket autouse `_short_timeout` fixture into two opt-in fixtures in\n   `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py`, both driving only the\n   existing test seams (`_acli_call_timeout`, `_ACLI_GRACE_SECONDS`, `_ACLI_DRAIN_SECONDS`) and\n   changing no production default:\n   - a reap-timing fixture installing sub-second call/grace/drain for the tests whose child is\n     required to HANG (a shorter deadline makes a hang MORE certain, never less);\n   - a generous hang-guard ceiling, far above any healthy run, for the tests whose child is\n     required to COMPLETE, so their pass condition stops depending on runner speed at all.\n2. Close the setup race instead of tuning around it. Every fake `python -c` binary writes a\n   readiness marker as its LAST action before hanging, and a parent-side `Popen` wrapper polls\n   for that marker (generous ceiling; early-out if the child exits) before handing the process\n   back. Because `_run_acli` measures its deadline at `communicate()`, which runs after the\n   wrapper returns, the injected window is spent entirely on the hang and can no longer\n   pre-empt the child's setup — on any host, at any load.\n3. Lower the injected low-speed window in `tight_stall` from 2s to 1s in\n   `tests/unit/test_snapshot_fetch_stall_abort_12e4.py`. The abort remains throughput-keyed and\n   the assertions remain reason-keyed (`\"stalled\" in message`, `\"timed out after\" not in\n   message`) under an unchanged 60s `_GIT_TIMEOUT` ceiling, so a slow host makes the test\n   slower, never red.\n4. Prove each touched sentinel still discriminates by seeding the defect it guards (restore the\n   production-scale window; disable the process-group reap; disable the timeout) and recording\n   RED, then reverting and recording GREEN.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Slow-progress still crosses a full configured low-speed window and proves throughput, not elapsed wall time, prevents abort.\n- [x] ACLI read still spawns exactly the production retry count and proves every process group is reaped.\n- [x] Production timeout/stall defaults are unchanged.\n- [x] The focused tests retain real subprocess/socket execution but complete in low single-digit seconds on macOS CI.\n- [x] No absolute upper-bound assertion is tightened into a loaded-runner flake.\n- [x] Every reap assertion that depends on child-side setup is ordered by an explicit readiness gate rather than by the injected window being larger than interpreter startup.\n- [x] Each touched sentinel is shown RED under a seeded defect and GREEN after revert.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `tests/unit/test_snapshot_fetch_stall_abort_12e4.py`\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py`"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787208679078140001,"uuid":"9f349b8c-c79d-4550-b916-5bfdee4be23b"}